message: "If you use this code or these results, please cite the archived Zenodo releases below."
title: "Why This Language? Historical Symmetry Breaking through Cultural Transmission: code, pre-registrations, results and manuscript"
type: software
version: 2.0.0
date-released: 2026-09-05
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22338797
url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22338797"
abstract: >-
  Research software, pre-registrations, results and manuscript for a minimal controlled
  model of cultural transmission in a Lewis referential game. A limited transmitted record
  breaks symmetry among multiple structured reconstructions available to independent
  learners, biasing them toward a shared, historically contingent organisation despite
  substantial item-level turnover; the record also biases reconstruction toward the
  developmental state in which it was captured (the Snapshot Effect). Pre-registered,
  replicated on untouched seeds, tested in a second architecture and in a minimal
  reconstruction model. Version 2.0.0 corrects Version 1.0.0 after an independent data
  verification.
keywords:
  - cultural transmission
  - iterated learning
  - emergent communication
  - symmetry breaking
  - developmental provenance
  - language evolution
  - referential game
  - pre-registration
  - reproducible research
